Software Development Team Leadership Strategy

1. Strategic Project Planning
As the team leader, my role is to oversee the project's who, what, where, when, and why. Following Sommerville (2016), I prioritize disciplined management
to ensure our software is dependable and delivered within our semester constraints.
      	•Scheduling: I develop the project timeline and identify the critical path to prevent bottlenecks between roles.
      	•Risk Management: I assess technical and schedule-related risks, implementing mitigation strategies to ensure project continuity.
	       •Resource Allocation: I delegate tasks based on member expertise while maintaining a balanced workload across the group.

2. Facilitating Integrated Team Roles
	•Requirements Analyst: I assist in defining user requirements and acting as a buffer against scope creep to keep the project's scale manageable.
	•Software Designer: I will collaborate on architectural scalability and help translate complex academic models (like the Process View) into practical 
   logic that the programmer can easily 	implement.
	•Programmer/Tester: Support Bandile by establishing technical standards for example version control or coding conventions and overseeing the integration of 
  unit and system testing to 	ensure a verified product.

3. Team Health and Quality Assurance
According to Sommerville (2016), "people management" is a key factor in success. My leadership focuses on:
	•Consistency and Communication: I facilitate daily check-ins to maintain a shared mental model 	of our progress. This ensures blunders are caught early and 
   addressed with constructive 	feedback.
	•Inclusion: I ensure the programmer’s technical constraints inform the requirements phase, while 	the analyst’s insights guide the testing phase.
	•Product Evolution: I am responsible for the final product quality. I manage to change requests 	to ensure requirements, design, and code stay synchronized 
   throughout the software lifecycle.

Summary of Result
By providing a structure where the Requirements Analyst defines the "what," the Software Designer blueprints the "how," and the Programmer/Tester executes the 
"result," I guide the team toward a reliable, professional software solution delivered on time.

